{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the points are the stationary points of the function"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the points are the stationary points of the function"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y-4x}{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,5)$\n- $(-2,-4)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,5)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(1,5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(5)-4(1)}{1} \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-4)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-2,-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-4)-4(-2)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-3)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-1,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-3)-4(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\  & =-1+0+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =(2{y}^{2})^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Given that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{(y)^{′}{x}^{2}-y({x}^{2})^{′}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}-2xy)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)} & ={\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-2(1))}{(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+2{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,-1)} & ={(4y+2{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =4(-1)+2(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4xy)|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(0)(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)\\cdot y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =4y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4y+4x(4xy) \\\\  & =4y(1+4{x}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4y(1+4{x}^{2}))|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(1)(1+4(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(0,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-89894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d9011c63223c561564c85189f9eff20b255ba6c608087adcce992ef3e8cfdee"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7924.json" "file_sha256" "95d0ec9300598e7c0b262528fc4f729ad43808a4e77e71f32d1f594de8857282"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7924.json" "file_sha256" "95d0ec9300598e7c0b262528fc4f729ad43808a4e77e71f32d1f594de8857282"}] "usage" {"e-7924" [] "q-89424" [] "e-7923" [] "q-89891" [] "q-89414" [] "e-7918" []} "basis" 1239 "retractions" [[17592186157916 :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $y^{′} - y = x^{3} + 3x^{2}$. Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(-1,-2)$\n2. $(1, 1)$\n3. $(1,-2)$"] [17592186157917 :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2y + x^{2}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}$ at $P$."] [17592186157929 :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = ye^{x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $(0, e)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(0, e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(0, e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(0, e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$"] [17592186157113 :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(0,0)$\n2. $(1,1)$\n3. $(-4,4)$"] [17592186157113 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(0, 0)} &= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(0, 0)} \\\\ = (0 + 0)^{3} \\\\ = 0✓ \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)}|= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)} \\\\ = (1 + 1)^{3} \\\\ = 8 \\\\ \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(-4, 4)}|= (y + x)^{3} \\mid _{(-4, 4)} \\\\\n&= (4 + (-4))^{3} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186157117 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P$."] [17592186157117 :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y - e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y) - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\ninto the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4(4y - e^{3x}) - 3e^{3x} \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 16y - 7e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(0, 1)} &= (16y - 7e^{3x}) \\mid _{(0, 1)} \\\\\n&= 16(1) - 7e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157118 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$"] [17592186157118 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0)$, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} - 4 - (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(4) - \\frac{d}{dx}(y) \\\\\n&= 2x - \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2x - (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= - (2)^{2} + 2(2) + 4 + (0) \\\\\n&= 4 \\\\\n&> 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0$,\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$.\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0)$. So, according to the second derivative test, since\n$\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} \\right|_{(2,0)} > 0$,\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156749 17592186156750 17592186156751 17592186156752 17592186156753 17592186156755 17592186156756 17592186156757 17592186156758 17592186156759 17592186156827 17592186156828 17592186156829 17592186156830 17592186156831] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156748 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156748 :answer-field/id] [17592186156748 :answer-field/key] [17592186156748 :ans